---
// PersonalSoftware.astro — "Not every app needs to become a service." (TASK-20260827)
//
// The section sits between the differentiators and the try-it split: by this point the
// reader knows the mechanism, and this is the consequence stated in human terms.
//
// CLAIMS DISCIPLINE — the whole reason this component has a comment. The defensible claim
// is that Snug removes the need for an APPLICATION-SPECIFIC SaaS backend accumulating the
// user's application state. It is NOT that external services disappear: a budget app still
// crosses an aggregator to reach a bank, and a hosted model still sees what is sent to it
// for inference. The closing line names both out loud rather than letting a reader infer a
// stronger claim and discover the caveat later — a reader who catches one overclaim stops
// believing the honest claims too (the standing rule, ADR-0059 rule 4).
//
// positioning.test.ts pins the "application-specific" scoping and asserts the absence of
// "never leaves your machine" / "no third party sees" / "100% private" here.
---

<section class="section personal" aria-labelledby="personal-title">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">personal software</p>
      <h2 id="personal-title">Not every app needs to become a service.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="personal-grid">
      <div class="personal-lede">
        <p>
          A budget tracker. A reading log. A personal CRM. A family organiser. A research tool for
          one recurring question. None of these are businesses — and none of them should need
          another account, another application-specific cloud database, and another company
          accumulating a permanent copy of what you put in.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Snug lets the application and its accumulated state live with you instead.</strong>
          The app runs against the intelligence your host provides, and everything it remembers
          stays in your <code>.snug</code> file — no backend of its own to sign up for, and nothing
          for a shutdown notice to take away.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="personal-honest">
        <p class="personal-honest-h">What that does and doesn't mean</p>
        <p>
          Snug removes the need for an <strong>application-specific</strong> SaaS backend
          accumulating your data. It does not remove every external service: an app that reads your
          bank still crosses an aggregator, and a hosted model still sees what an app sends it for
          inference. What changes is <strong>who ends up holding the accumulated picture</strong> —
          and with local inference, how little has to leave at all.
        </p>
        <p class="personal-more">
          <a href="/architecture/">See where the data actually sits — Fig 4 →</a>
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

